<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danegeld</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Danegeld was a tax raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to pay tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders, above all from 991 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a>, when large sums were paid to Danish armies to stop their attacks. Later the name was used for a land tax collected by English kings, including under Cnut and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>, until the twelfth century. Much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> flowed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danegeld.jpg" alt="Danegeld" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berig · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U_241,_Lingsberg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
